window.docContents[209].resolve({"/#Alternative":{"contents":"Many types support a notion of failure and recovery.\nThe  monad from the section on evaluating arithmetic expressions in a variety of monads is one such type, as is .\nBoth support failure without providing a reason (unlike, say,  and , which require some indication of what went wrong).\n\nThe  class describes applicative functors that have additional operators for failure and recovery:\n\n\n\nJust as implementors of  get  instances for free, implementors of  get  instances for free:\n\n\n\nThe implementation of  for  keeps the first non- argument:\n\n\n\nSimilarly, the implementation for  follows the general structure of , with minor differences due to the laziness-inducing  parameters being placed differently:\n\n\n\nLike other type classes,  enables the definition of a variety of operations that work for any applicative functor that implements .\nOne of the most important is , which causes  when a decidable proposition is false:\n\n\n\nIt is very useful in monadic programs to terminate execution early.\nIn , it can be used to filter out a whole branch of a search, as in the following program that computes all even divisors of a natural number:\n\n\n\nRunning it on  yields the expected results:\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009Alternatives","header":"5.4.2. The  Alternative  Class","id":"/#Alternative"},"/#example-cat-running":{"contents":"To check whether feline works, the first step is to build it with lake build.\nFirst off, when called without arguments, it should emit what it receives from standard input.\nCheck thatecho \"It works!\" | lake exe feline\nemits It works!.\n\nSecondly, when called with files as arguments, it should print them.\nIf the file test1.txt containsIt's time to find a warm spot\nand test2.txt containsand curl up!\nthen the commandlake exe feline test1.txt test2.txtshould emitIt's time to find a warm spot\nand curl up!\n\n\nFinally, the - argument should be handled appropriately.\n\necho \"and purr\" | lake exe feline test1.txt - test2.txt\n\n\nshould yield\n\nIt's time to find a warm spot\nand purr\nand curl up!\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!\u0009Worked Example:  cat","header":"2.4.3. Meow!","id":"/#example-cat-running"},"/#or-patterns":{"contents":"In contexts that allow multiple patterns, such as match-expressions, multiple patterns may share their result expressions.\nThe datatype  that represents days of the week:\n\n\n\nPattern matching can be used to check whether a day is a weekend:\n\n\n\nThis can already be simplified by using constructor dot notation:\n\n\n\nBecause both weekend patterns have the same result expression (), they can be condensed into one:\n\n\n\nThis can be further simplified into a version in which the argument is not named:\n\n\n\nBehind the scenes, the result expression is simply duplicated across each pattern.\nThis means that patterns can bind variables, as in this example that removes the  and  constructors from a sum type in which both contain the same type of value:\n\n\n\nBecause the result expression is duplicated, the variables bound by the patterns are not required to have the same types.\nOverloaded functions that work for multiple types may be used to write a single result expression that works for patterns that bind variables of different types:\n\n\n\nIn practice, only variables shared in all patterns can be referred to in the result expression, because the result must make sense for each pattern.\nIn , only  can be accessed, and attempts to use either  or  lead to errors.\n\n\n\nAttempting to access  in a similar definition causes an error because there is no  available in the second pattern:\n\n\n\n\n\nThe fact that the result expression is essentially copy-pasted to each branch of the pattern match can lead to some surprising behavior.\nFor example, the following definitions are acceptable because the  version of the result expression refers to the global definition of :\n\n\n\nCalling this function on both constructors reveals the confusing behavior.\nIn the first case, a type annotation is needed to tell Lean which type  should be:\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the second case, the global definition is used:\n\n\n\n\n\nUsing or-patterns can vastly simplify some definitions and increase their clarity, as in .\nBecause there is a potential for confusing behavior, it's a good idea to be careful when using them, especially when variables of multiple types or disjoint sets of variables are involved.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monads\u0009Additional Conveniences","header":"4.6.3. Or-Patterns","id":"/#or-patterns"},"/#subtypes":{"contents":"Representing these conditions is easiest with one additional Lean type, called :\n\n\n\nThis structure has two type parameters: an implicit parameter that is the type of data , and an explicit parameter  that is a predicate over .\nA predicate is a logical statement with a variable in it that can be replaced with a value to yield an actual statement, like the parameter to  that describes what it means for an index to be in bounds for a lookup.\nIn the case of , the predicate slices out some subset of the values of  for which the predicate holds.\nThe structure's two fields are, respectively, a value from  and evidence that the value satisfies the predicate .\nLean has special syntax for .\nIf  has type , then the type  can also be written , or even  when the type  can be inferred automatically.\n\nRepresenting positive numbers as inductive types is clear and easy to program with.\nHowever, it has a key disadvantage.\nWhile  and  have the structure of ordinary inductive types from the perspective of Lean programs, the compiler treats them specially and uses fast arbitrary-precision number libraries to implement them.\nThis is not the case for additional user-defined types.\nHowever, a subtype of  that restricts it to non-zero numbers allows the new type to use the efficient representation while still ruling out zero at compile time:\n\n\n\nThe smallest fast positive number is still one.\nNow, instead of being a constructor of an inductive type, it's an instance of a structure that's constructed with angle brackets.\nThe first argument is the underlying , and the second argument is the evidence that said  is greater than zero:\n\n\n\nThe proposition  is decidable, so the decide tactic produces the necessary evidence.\nThe  instance is very much like that for , except it uses a short tactic proof to provide evidence that n + 1 > 0:\n\n\n\nHere, simp is needed because decide requires concrete values, but the proposition in question is .\n\nSubtypes are a two-edged sword.\nThey allow efficient representation of validation rules, but they transfer the burden of maintaining these rules to the users of the library, who have to prove that they are not violating important invariants.\nGenerally, it's a good idea to use them internally to a library, providing an API to users that automatically ensures that all invariants are satisfied, with any necessary proofs being internal to the library.\n\nChecking whether a value of type  is in the subtype  usually requires that the proposition  be decidable.\nThe section on equality and ordering classes describes how decidable propositions can be used with if.\nWhen if is used with a decidable proposition, a name can be provided.\nIn the then branch, the name is bound to evidence that the proposition is true, and in the else branch, it is bound to evidence that the proposition is false.\nThis comes in handy when checking whether a given  is positive:\n\n\n\nIn the then branch,  is bound to evidence that , and this evidence can be used as the second argument to 's constructor.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009Applicative Functors\u0009A Non-Monadic Applicative","header":"5.2.1.2. Subtypes","id":"/#subtypes"}});